---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
import { docHref, SITE } from "../lib/site";

const UDEV_RULE =
  'SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="0bb4", ATTR{idProduct}=="0c01", TAG+="uaccess"';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Install from the browser"
  description="Connect your Echo over USB and check it from the browser. Nothing is written to the device."
  slug="install"
>
  <div class="pn-notice" data-tone="caution">
    <Icon name="shield" class="pn-notice-icon" />
    <div class="pn-notice-body">
      <p class="pn-notice-title !my-0">This page only checks the device for now</p>
      <p class="!my-0">
        It reads what the device is and whether Pinna can be installed on it.
        It writes nothing. To install Pinna, follow
        <a class="link" href={docHref("flash")}>Install Pinna</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div id="install-unsupported" class="pn-notice mt-5" data-tone="danger" hidden>
    <Icon name="close" class="pn-notice-icon" />
    <div class="pn-notice-body">
      <p class="pn-notice-title !my-0">This browser can't talk to USB devices</p>
      <p class="!my-0">
        Open this page in Chrome, Edge, Brave or Opera on a computer. Firefox
        and Safari don't support WebUSB.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>What you need</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Chrome, Edge, Brave or Opera on a computer.</li>
    <li>
      For the Echo Dot (2nd gen): a micro-USB cable that carries data, plugged
      straight into the computer, not into a hub.
    </li>
    <li>
      For the Echo (2nd gen): a connection to the USB pads on its base, with
      pogo pins or a soldered cable. The device has no USB port.
    </li>
    <li>
      On Linux: a udev rule that lets your user open the device. See
      <a class="link" href="#cannot-open">The browser can't open the device</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      On Windows: the WinUSB driver for the device. See
      <a class="link" href="#busy">The browser can't use the device</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Check your device</h2>

  <form id="install-check" class="not-prose">
    <fieldset class="flex flex-col gap-2">
      <legend class="text-ink mb-2 font-semibold">1. Choose your device</legend>
      <label class="flex items-center gap-2">
        <input type="radio" name="model" value="echo-dot-2" required />
        Echo Dot (2nd gen)
      </label>
      <label class="flex items-center gap-2">
        <input type="radio" name="model" value="echo-2" />
        Echo (2nd gen)
      </label>
    </fieldset>
  </form>

  <p class="text-ink mt-6 mb-2 font-semibold">2. Start the device in fastboot</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Unplug the device.</li>
    <li>
      Connect it to the computer: the Echo Dot (2nd gen) with its micro-USB
      cable, the Echo (2nd gen) with the pins on its USB pads.
    </li>
    <li>
      Hold the button for its bootloader, and plug in the power while you hold
      it: on the Echo Dot (2nd gen) that is the micro-USB cable itself, on the
      Echo (2nd gen) its power adapter.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Volume Down (−)</strong> if the device was unlocked before:
          it runs Pinna or the previous firmware.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Action</strong> if it was never unlocked: it still runs
          Amazon's software.
        </li>
      </ul>
      If you are not sure, try Volume Down first, then Action.
    </li>
    <li>Release the button after a few seconds.</li>
  </ol>

  <p class="text-ink mt-6 mb-2 font-semibold">3. Connect</p>
  <p>
    The browser asks which device to use. Choose <strong>Android</strong>
    (<code>0bb4:0c01</code>).
  </p>
  <div class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
    <button id="install-connect" type="button" class="pn-btn pn-btn-primary" disabled>
      Connect
    </button>
    <p id="install-status" class="text-ink-muted !my-0 text-sm" role="status" aria-live="polite">
      Choose your device first.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div id="install-problem" class="pn-notice mt-5" data-tone="danger" hidden>
    <Icon name="close" class="pn-notice-icon" />
    <div class="pn-notice-body">
      <p id="install-problem-title" class="pn-notice-title !my-0"></p>
      <p id="install-problem-text" class="!my-0"></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section id="install-result" class="card mt-6 p-5" hidden>
    <p id="install-headline" class="text-ink !mt-0 text-lg font-semibold"></p>
    <p id="install-detail" class="text-ink-muted"></p>
    <p id="install-next" class="text-ink"></p>
    <dl id="install-facts" class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-[max-content_minmax(0,1fr)] gap-x-6 gap-y-1 text-sm"></dl>

    <p class="text-ink mt-6 mb-2 font-semibold">Report</p>
    <p class="!mt-0 text-sm">
      Paste this into an issue if the result is wrong, or if the device's
      bootloader is unknown. The serial number is cut short.
    </p>
    <textarea
      id="install-report"
      class="pn-input is-mono h-56 w-full py-2"
      readonly
      aria-label="Device report"></textarea>
    <div class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <button id="install-copy" type="button" class="pn-btn">Copy</button>
      <a id="install-issue" class="pn-btn" href={`${SITE.repo}/issues/new`}>
        Open an issue
        <Icon name="external" size={14} />
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2>If it doesn't work</h2>

  <h3>The device isn't in the list</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The device isn't in fastboot. Start it again as described in step 2, and
      try the other button.
    </li>
    <li>
      The cable only charges. Try another cable, and a USB port on the
      computer itself.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="cannot-open">The browser can't open the device</h3>
  <p>
    On Linux the browser runs as your user, and only root may open USB
    devices that no rule names. Add this rule as
    <code>/etc/udev/rules.d/60-pinna-fastboot.rules</code>:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{UDEV_RULE}</code></pre>
  <p>Then load it, and unplug and plug in the device again:</p>
  <pre><code>sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger</code></pre>
  <p>
    On NixOS, put the rule in <code>services.udev.extraRules</code> instead.
  </p>

  <h3 id="busy">The browser can't use the device</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Another program uses it: <code>fastboot</code>, <code>adb</code> (run
      <code>adb kill-server</code>), Android Studio, or this page in another
      tab. Close them and connect again.
    </li>
    <li>
      On Windows the browser needs the WinUSB driver for the device. Install
      it with <a class="link" href="https://zadig.akeo.ie/">Zadig</a>: choose
      <strong>Options › List All Devices</strong>, select
      <strong>Android</strong> (USB ID <code>0BB4 0C01</code>), choose
      <strong>WinUSB</strong> and <strong>Install Driver</strong>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="no-reply">The device doesn't answer</h3>
  <p>
    The page stops after the first request that gets no reply, and sends
    nothing more. Some devices that were never unlocked reset when they get a
    request and come back in fastboot. Unplug the device, start it in fastboot
    again, use another cable or USB port, and connect again. If it happens
    every time, open an issue and say which device and computer you use.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>

<script>
  import { startCheckPage } from "../lib/installer/check-page";
  startCheckPage();
</script>
